From d174b7c67a99f6b8c219d0119cd22aafc0873264 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:38:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: say why native Dolby Vision is not used

When the kernel refused native Dolby Vision output, Kodi learnt only
the errno of the commit: the player information then said "failed;
tried again with a later video", and the log asked for the kernel log.
The kernel patches of this series give the reasons of a refusal as a
read-only bitmask connector property, DVBRIDGE_REFUSED (i915 and amdgpu
with the Hades patches: converter, sink, tmds, ycbcr420, frl, edid,
edid-2160p30, disabled, format, mode, clone, scanout, other).

- CDRMObject reads a property as the kernel has it now
  (ReadPropertyValue()) and the names of the bits of a bitmask property
  (GetPropertyBitmaskNames()).
- CDRMAtomic reads DVBRIDGE_REFUSED after each refused transport commit
  (the test or the commit itself) and keeps what it names until a
  transport commit is accepted or the output is captured again; the
  error line of the refusal names it.
- When native output is disabled after a failure, the reason code of
  the player information comes from it (DVBRIDGE::REFUSED::Code(), pure,
  with a host test): converter, sink, frl, link (TMDS or YCbCr 4:2:0
  only), driveredid, rate, output or refused (the driver refused what
  Kodi asked for; the kernel log says what it found). Without a reason
  from the driver it is "failed", as before.

Two cases never reach a request, and are now named before it:
- the display is on a DisplayPort output (the TV behind an LSPCON on the
  board, as on many Intel desktops with an MCDP2800, or behind a
  DisplayPort or USB-C to HDMI adapter): "converter" instead of "the
  video output does not support it";
- the output mode is not a transport mode and the output offers none
  (an HDMI 1.4 port, cable, adapter or TV input, or a DisplayPort
  dual-mode adapter of the first type): "nomode".
The i915/xe rule of the Dolby Vision block (12-byte version 2 only,
without an EEODB) gets its own code, "driveredid".

Nothing that decides whether native output is used changes: only the
code and the text of the reason, and the log lines.

On top of the overview page of the player information ("estuary: an
overview page in the player information, fed from the real output
state"), which shows the reason in a row of 590 pixels (font12): four
reasons are shorter, so that each fits there and in the reason row of
the Dolby Vision page (885 pixels with "Reason: ", font14) in English
and in Spanish, measured with Estuary's font as that page's evaluation
measures it. Their first texts went past one room or both (up to 735
and 1094 pixels for "refused" in Spanish): "the TV is on a DisplayPort
output or converter", "the link cannot carry this mode in 8-bit RGB",
"the graphics driver rejects this TV's DV block" and "refused by the
driver (see the kernel log)", with their Spanish.
